@page "/docs/components/virtualizer"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.Virtualizer
<PageTitle>Virtualizer - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Virtualizer</h1>
            <DocBadges Slug="virtualizer" />
        </div>
        <p>
            Renders only the items in view, so a list of thousands stays light in the DOM. Feed it from
            <code>Items</code>, a list you own, or from an <code>ItemsProvider</code> that fetches each
            window on demand. Either way, it renders the visible slice plus two spacers that keep the
            scroll height exact.
        </p>
        <p>
            By default it virtualizes against the page with no height cap, and <code>MaxHeight</code>
            turns it into a fixed scroll box. <code>BzDataTable&lt;TItem&gt;</code> uses this same
            component to virtualize its rows.
        </p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(VirtualizerUsageExample)"
                Description="Pass `Items` and an item template. The template's context is a `VirtualItem`: render `context.Item` and apply `context.Attributes` to the root element. Set `MaxHeight` for a fixed scroll box, or leave it out to virtualize against the page." />

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Loading from a server</h2>
        <p>
            Two parameters cover async data, and they suit different kinds of list. Pick one, because
            setting both throws.
        </p>
        <BzTable>
            <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Parameter</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Who owns the data</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Memory</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Reach for it when</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap align-top"><code>Items</code> and <code>OnLoadMore</code></BzTableCell>
                    <BzTableCell Class="align-top">You do. Each batch is appended to your own collection and stays there, and the callback fires as the user nears the end.</BzTableCell>
                    <BzTableCell Class="align-top">Grows with everything that has been scrolled past.</BzTableCell>
                    <BzTableCell Class="align-top">Feeds, chats and activity logs: lists read in order, with modest totals and data you want to keep around to search or reuse.</BzTableCell>
                </BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap align-top"><code>ItemsProvider</code></BzTableCell>
                    <BzTableCell Class="align-top">The virtualizer does. It requests each window as the user scrolls, cancels stale requests and discards what scrolls away. Call <code>RefreshDataAsync</code> to query again when your data changes.</BzTableCell>
                    <BzTableCell Class="align-top">Constant: one window of a few dozen items, whatever the total.</BzTableCell>
                    <BzTableCell Class="align-top">Large server data sets where the user jumps around with the scrollbar, and totals too big to hold in memory, or in every circuit on Blazor Server.</BzTableCell>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>
            As a rule of thumb, use <code>OnLoadMore</code> when the user <i>reads through</i> the list,
            and <code>ItemsProvider</code> when the user <i>jumps around</i> in it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Fixed height"
          Description="When every item is exactly `ItemSize` tall, placement is plain index arithmetic, which scales to huge lists. Here 100,000 rows scroll inside a 20rem box while only the visible rows are in the DOM."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerFixedExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Dynamic height"
          Description="Set `Dynamic` when items differ in height, such as the wrapping notes here. The virtualizer measures each rendered item, and `ItemSize` becomes the estimate used until an item has been measured. In this mode `context.Attributes` carries only the index marker, not a fixed height."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerDynamicExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Empty state"
          Description="When `Items` is empty, the virtualizer renders `EmptyContent` in place of the rows and spacers."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerEmptyExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Loading"
          Description="Set `Loading` while a fetch is in flight so an empty list reads as loading rather than as no results. By default the virtualizer shows item-sized skeleton rows, and `LoadingContent` replaces them with your own placeholder. Once items arrive, the rows render as usual whatever the flag says."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerLoadingExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Infinite loading"
          Description="`Items` with `OnLoadMore` suits a feed, where batches build up in a list you own. When the rendered window comes within `LoadMoreOffset` items of the end, 10 by default, the callback fires once and skeleton rows fill the tail until you append the next batch. When the source runs dry, return without appending and no further fetch is made."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerInfiniteExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Server data (ItemsProvider)"
          Description="Half a million rows with constant memory. For each window the user scrolls to, the provider receives a start index and count and returns that slice plus the total, while skeleton rows or your own `Placeholder` stand in. Because the total sizes the scrollbar up front, dragging it to the middle fetches only that window. Call `RefreshDataAsync` after the underlying data changes."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerProviderExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Scroll to an item"
          Description="`InitialItemIndex` opens a long list part way down on the first render. After that, call `ScrollToIndexAsync` through a component reference to jump to any item instantly, aligned to the top. In `Dynamic` mode, a jump into unmeasured items lands on the `ItemSize` estimate and settles as they are measured."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerScrollToExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Customization"
          Description="`Class` is merged onto the virtualizer root, so you can style the scroll box however you like, and rows take their look from your template. This example stripes alternate rows using `row.Index`."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerCustomExample)" />

    <Demo Title="RTL"
          Description="Inside a right-to-left `DirectionProvider`, the rows lay out from the right with no extra work, because nothing in the virtualizer depends on direction. The logical `ms-auto` class pushes the email to the inline end, which is now the left edge."
          Example="typeof(VirtualizerRtlExample)" />

</div>

